Vector FP on the shared multi-cycle FPU (branch timing-vecfp, 2026-10-16)
========================================================================

Base: fork main 910c023 (L1 caches, PR #15). Host load ~75-130 on 48 cores,
so wall times are pessimistic.

== Per-block synthesis (scripts/synth_block_orfs.sh) ==
ORFS Yosys flow of the full-SoC run (slang, ORFS abc script), Nangate45
typical, OpenSTA on the unrepaired netlist with the liberty wire-load model,
10 ns clock, zero I/O delays. "before" = `git archive origin/main rtl`
(2789d95; muserisc_vec/fpu sources are unchanged by 910c023).

block                       rev     area (um^2)  worst reg->reg path                     delay
muserisc_vec                before  148,417.6    op_q[1] -> w_d_q[56]                    125.906 ns
                                                 (combinational fp_eval incl. div/sqrt)
muserisc_vec                after    59,282.9    sew_q[0] -> mres_q[51] (multiplier)       3.249 ns
muserisc_fpu (unchanged)            51,132.9    d_q -> res_q[43] (round)                   3.562 ns
muserisc_vec_fpu_synth      after   110,925.2    u_fpu.d_q -> u_vec.fp_res[60]             4.161 ns
  (flow/vec-synth wrapper: vec + shared FPU + the core's 2:1 port mux)
  worst in->reg 1.474 ns (s_d_i -> u_fpu.ub_q), reg->out 4.143 ns
Wall: vec before 3,088 s, after 226 s; wrapper 483 s; FPU 199 s.

Area: before, vec (148,418) + scalar FPU (51,133) = 199,551 um^2; after,
vec + shared FPU + mux = 110,925 um^2 (-88,626). A dedicated vector FPU
instance would have added ~51k um^2 on top of the after-vec 59k.

The SoC report (docs/SOC_AREA.md on soc-sram-synth) has the same before
path: 128.0 ns, s2_step_q -> w_d_q, muserisc_vec own area 148,018 um^2.
The scalar FPU there closes at 7.6 ns because its EX operands come through
the core's forwarding network, which this block view does not include.

== Bit-exactness ==
Simulation asserts every vector FPU completion against fp_eval and every
vfrec7/vfrsqrt7 estimate against fp_estimate on the issued operands
(muserisc_vec), in addition to the FPU's own cross-check; no assertion
fired in any run below.

